# BACKLOG — The openOODA-tools fleet repositories that hold no implementation

File: openOODA/docs/tools-backlog.oot

Status: v1.0.0 (2026-11-01) — 152 repositories tracked, none implemented.
Rule: rules.oot 1.8, 1.10, 1.14. Contract: RFC 0010 section 2.3, RFC 0011.
Gate: every repository below must report NO_BUILD_TARGET. Nothing here is a
success. A repository leaves this board only when it builds, when its parity
gate passes against the upstream tool, and when it reports PASS twice.

## 1. Purpose

The openOODA-tools organization holds 258 repositories. 104 of them ship a
real tool. The other 152 hold a `main.oo` and an `anchor.oo`, import nothing,
and answer every request with a canned reply. They must not pass. They must
also not be silent. This board names every one of them, the program each one
is meant to replace, and the tier that decides the order of the work.

## 2. The rule

A repository on this board is a backlog state, never a pass. The fleet gate
reports NO_BUILD_TARGET for it. The workflow of every repository on this board
fails on the missing build target. The published catalog marks it `planned`
rather than leaving it absent, so the public site states the truth about the
whole organization and not only about the shipped part.

## 3. The upstream contract

The `Upstream` column names the program the tool must replace. The goal is a
1:1 drop-in replacement: the same arguments, the same output, the same exit
code. The parity gate of every shipped tool compares it against that program.
A repository whose upstream is `none` is an openOODA tool and not a drop-in
replacement, so no parity gate runs against it.

## 4. The tiers

The tier order comes from RFC 0010 section 2.3. The network tier comes first
because a network tool holds the largest blast radius. The filesystem tier
comes second because a filesystem tool changes what the user sees. The rest
of the fleet follows in alphabetical order.

- `network` — the tool talks to the network or to another host.
- `fs` — the tool reads or writes the filesystem.
- `rest` — the tool computes or transforms text and data.

